# 1. Container / Presentational Pattern

## Definition

* Container components handle state, logic, and side effects
* Presentational components handle UI rendering only

## Strict Rules

❌ Presentational components MUST NOT contain business logic or side effects
❌ UI components MUST NOT directly manage external state (API calls, global stores)

## Use when:

* UI and logic need clear separation
* Component complexity grows beyond simple rendering

## Avoid when:

* Component is small and self-contained
* Separation creates unnecessary file fragmentation

---

# 2. Custom Hooks Pattern

## Definition

* Hooks encapsulate reusable stateful logic and side effects
* Hooks do NOT render UI

## Strict Rules

❌ Hooks MUST NOT return JSX or render logic
❌ Hooks MUST NOT contain UI concerns

## Use when:

* Logic is reused across multiple components
* Side effects (fetching, subscriptions, state logic) must be isolated

## Avoid when:

* Logic is local and simple
* Extracting hook reduces readability

---

# 3. Adapter Pattern

## Definition

* Adapters normalize external API data into internal UI-friendly models
* UI consumes only normalized data structures

## Strict Rules

❌ UI MUST NOT depend on raw API response structures
❌ API shapes MUST NOT leak into presentation layer

## Use when:

* External APIs are unstable or inconsistent
* Data requires transformation before UI consumption

## Avoid when:

* API is already stable, consistent, and UI-ready

---

# 4. Compound Components Pattern

## Definition

* A pattern for building composable UI systems using implicit state sharing
* Enables flexible composition (e.g., Modal, Dropdown, Tabs)

## Strict Rules

❌ Compound components MUST remain composable (no rigid coupling)
❌ Internal state sharing MUST NOT break external flexibility

## Use when:

* Building complex, multi-part UI systems
* Multiple UI parts must work together cohesively

## Avoid when:

* UI is simple or static
* Composition adds unnecessary complexity

---

# 5. Composition Over Inheritance

## Definition

* UI behavior should be built by composing components, not extending them

## Strict Rules

❌ Inheritance-based UI hierarchies SHOULD NOT be used
❌ Components SHOULD NOT rely on class inheritance for reuse

## Use when:

* Reusing behavior across multiple UI components
* Building flexible and reusable UI structures

## Avoid when:

* A single component already satisfies the requirement

---

# 6. Pattern Selection Rule (STRICT)

Patterns are NOT default solutions.

## MUST follow:

* Prefer simple implementations first
* Introduce patterns ONLY when they reduce real complexity
* Avoid abstraction without measurable benefit

## FORBIDDEN:

❌ Introducing patterns “just in case”
❌ Over-engineering simple components
❌ Adding abstraction without current need

## MUST introduce patterns only when they:

* Reduce complexity
* Improve maintainability
* Reduce duplication
* Clarify separation of concerns

---

# Summary Principle

* Containers separate logic from UI
* Hooks isolate reusable behavior
* Adapters isolate external data from UI
* Compound components enable flexible composition
* Composition is preferred over inheritance

> Simplicity first. Patterns only when necessary.
